king that person themselves in  egyptian mythology  statues were thought of as spiritual  vessels so removing a nose  was a way to disable that connection to  the afterlife but blyberg also theorizes  about another culprit for this  destruction  early christian monks every new society  when it's creating  destroys images of the past  you see the same thing happening in the  french revolution  with the overthrow of the aristocratic  government of france  there was also an overthrow of statues  this is a cyclical process and it's  happened countless times throughout  history  once a society rises it builds statues  to represent itself  and then as a new society comes along it  removes or destroys those old statues  and replaces them with their own an  example of that cycle on a shorter  timeline  are the christopher columbus statues  across the united states  statues of columbus are fascinating to  me because they were  put up at a time when italians were not  necessarily considered white when  whiteness was something that was  northern european statues of columbus  were  funded by italian-american groups as a  part of an argument that we  are italian-american don't reject us  don't discriminate against us  now statues of columbus are being  protested by native americans who see  columbus as a symbol  of the fact that they are excluded from  belonging to america  and while the facts around columbus as a  man remain problematic  as a symbol he simultaneously stands for  mass genocide to some  and national pride to others  what these heritage sites and places  statues and  and historic buildings do is they give  us a  tangible sense of the intangible this is  professor laura jane smith  she spent her career researching  studying and writing about heritage and  its effects on society  including a decade-long study and how we  interact with heritage sites  most people go to reinforce what they  already know feel and believe  and this is one of the issues with the  heritage debate is that we have this  sort of sense that  we heritage particularly national  heritage  has to make us feel good and feel good  about ourselves  and when we visit these sites to  reinforce our deeply held beliefs  it can blind us to realities right under  our noses  smith pointed to a series of interviews  she conducted at andrew jackson's  hermitage in tennessee where visitors  had to walk through an  exhibit on the former president's role  in slavery before they could enter his  palatial plantation house  visitors engaged in an array of  responses that  attempted to shield themselves from the  from the  negative emotions that cut into their  sense of comfort for visiting the pres  this presidential house  some people would say when i asked them  what did you think of the exhibition  would say what exhibition i didn't  notice  any exhibition so intent were they to  get to the comforting presidential  performance  others would go oh yes i saw it but i'm  not in you know it's not relevant  it's not relevant to this to this  history i've come to see  jackson while it may be natural for many  of us to avoid engaging with the more  complicated parts of our history these  public forms of art  are having an impact on us as a society  neuroscientists talk about the halo  effect and i like to talk about  the pedestal effect  when we see someone up on a pedestal all  these assumptions kick in that they must  be someone who's there to be respected  to be praised to be honored  so statues are very bad at communicating  nuance and shades of gray  they are what michael billig has  referred to as a form of banal  nationalism  they are so ubiquitous that we tend to  forget that they're there but  nonetheless are constantly referencing  and representing particular ideas and  and  group identity and so on identity is  complicated it's  much easier to think of america as a set  of almost like flashes of images of  the flag and the statue of liberty not a  portrait of washington  and that is a shorthand for what it  means to be an american  so if you change those images that  changes identity images  summarize complex things and if we want  to change complex things sometimes we  need to change those summary images  when we're not fighting actual wars we  often fight wars by using images  by attacking the idea of what the other  side is standing for  to say i don't want a white supremacist  america anymore you attack statues that  represent a white supremacist america  but watching these images change in real  time is much different than walking  through an  ancient iconoclasm exhibit in a museum  while removing or reclaiming a statue  can give some that same cathartic sense  as removing an ex from a photo  for others it feels like a personal  attack  not only on their past but their future  if you don't  pay attention to history it repeats  itself through history people have been  horrified by the destruction  of statues because that's a sign of our  fragility that not only our bodies but  our whole legacy  any memory of us could be overthrown  disrespected  disregarded so to defend a statue  of really anybody is to defend this idea  that you  yourself might be around for the future  heritage  and history are constant processes of  reassessing who we are  and who we want to be debate will always  be ongoing  and that that in itself is i think a far  more healthy and constructive  way of approaching both heritage and  history

